WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.110
Imagine, for a moment, that you were given
the opportunity to become the most powerful

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.770
person in your country.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:10.820
To get there, all you need to do is betray
the very man who made you who you are.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:15.429
Would you demure, step back, say “thanks,
but no thanks”?

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:19.060
Or would you grab that opportunity with both
hands?

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.789
Nearly half a century ago, one man in Chile
made the decision to betray everything for

00:00:23.789 --> 00:00:25.100
his own glory.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:34.039
His name was Augusto Pinochet.

00:00:34.039 --> 00:00:38.030
The eldest son of middle class Catholics,
Pinochet spent the first fifty eight years

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:42.620
of his life wallowing in obscurity, only to
suddenly rise to infamy.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:48.300
The driving force of the 1973 coup that deposed
Chile’s democratically elected president,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.980
Pinochet oversaw a reign of terror almost
unmatched on the continent.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:58.579
Yet he was also a leader who counted icons
like Margaret Thatcher among his closest friends.

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:04.470
Join us today as we delve into the murky life
of the man who boosted Chile’s economy,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:09.020
even as he eviscerated its soul.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:15.460
The Donkey
In the lives of evil men, there are often

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:19.990
telling details from their early years that
show the monsters they will become.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:25.140
The serial killer who tortures animals; the
future dictator who delights in suffering.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.220
In the early life Augusto Pinochet Ugarte,
however, there was nothing.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:34.950
Nothing to suggest this young boy would one
day drown his nation in a sea of blood.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:39.880
Born on November 25, 1915 in Chile’s great
port city of Valparaiso, baby Augusto came

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.229
from a solidly middle class background.

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:46.950
His father, Augusto Snr., was a local customs
official descended from French settlers, while

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.450
his mother, Avelina, was deeply religious.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:53.149
Speaking of Avelina, the child Pinochet was
something of a mommy’s boy.

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:58.689
When most of the family moved out to the countryside,
leaving Avelina in Valparaiso, Pinochet demanded

00:01:58.689 --> 00:01:59.689
to stay with her.

00:01:59.689 --> 00:02:02.790
Still, it’s not like this familial separation
constituted “hardship.”

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.729
Pinochet’s early life was remarkably privileged.

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:10.010
He was even enrolled in Sagrados Corazones
private school, then one of Chile’s most

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:11.720
elite institutions.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.370
Not that this stopped other boys from picking
on him.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.410
As a teenager, the boy was, by his own admission,
woefully weak.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:21.440
He had a loud, braying laugh that earned him
the nickname “donkey”.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.440
He was also a pretty terrible student.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.640
One of the only things teenage Pinochet was
really good at was art, netting himself school

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.310
prizes for his paintings.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:34.590
But if Pinochet ever seriously flirted with
being an artist, the dream didn’t last long.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.120
By the time he graduated, the boy had decided
he was going to be a soldier.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.400
This was easier said than done.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.220
Pinochet’s father, Augusto Snr., was determined
his oldest son become a doctor.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:48.040
But Pinochet had an ace up his sleeve.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.770
Avelina doted on her eldest son as intensely
as Pinochet revered her.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:57.190
When the boy said he wanted to be a soldier,
Avelina made damn sure a soldier was what

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:58.190
he became.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:02:59.190
It was a rocky road.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.680
The military actually rejected his application
twice.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.090
But finally, in 1933, the boy and his mother’s
persistence paid off.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.160
Pinochet was accepted to the War Academy in
Santiago.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.880
The remarkable thing about Pinochet’s time
there is just how unremarkable it was.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.690
By the time Pinochet graduated with a commission
in the infantry, WWII was already looming.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:23.160
But since Chile decided to sit the war out
– only symbolically declaring war on Nazi

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.730
Germany during the endgame – Pinochet spent
his best fighting years kicking his heels

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:28.970
in Santiago.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:33.150
Still, even way down south in Chile, WWII
had a potent effect.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:38.390
The role of the USSR in fascism’s defeat
galvanized Communist parties in Latin America.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:42.080
Governments responded by cracking down on
them, sometimes using extreme measures.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.140
By 1948, the Chilean government was keeping
Communists in internment camps.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:51.121
It was while overseeing one of these camps
that Pinochet had his first run-in with the

00:03:51.121 --> 00:03:52.121
Doctor.

00:03:52.121 --> 00:03:57.230
That year, an upstart Socialist senator decided
to make a personal inspection of the internment

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.540
camps, to see if the Communists were being
treated well.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.160
When he arrived at Pinochet’s camp, the
man who’d once been known as the Donkey

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.160
threatened to shoot the senator.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:08.570
That senator’s name was Doctor Salvador
Allende.

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:13.900
Neither the Doctor nor the Donkey knew it
yet, but their fates were already inextricably

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.100
linked.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:22.900
The Doctor
The story of Augusto Pinochet isn’t the

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.420
story of one life so much as it is of two
lives.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:32.390
The life of Pinochet himself… and the life
of Dr. Salvador Allende.

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:36.980
Born on June 26, 1908, the Doctor’s early
life was eerily similar to the Donkey’s.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:41.190
Like Pinochet, Allende was likely born in
the Chilean port of Valparaiso – although

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.160
we’ve seen at least one source claim he
was really born in the capital, Santiago.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:49.570
What’s certainly true is that Allende also
came from a large, middle class family with

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.800
a devoutly religious matriarch.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.060
Like Pinochet, Allende’s father wanted him
to become a doctor.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:59.990
But while Pinochet’s formative experiences
would be shaped by the military, Allende’s

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:02.570
came from a very different direction.

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:07.830
Juan Demarchi is one of those figures who
exist on the sidelines of history, yet still

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:11.000
manage to exert an outsized pull on events.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:16.030
An Italian immigrant cobbler, Demarchi lived
on the same street as the Allendes.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:19.040
But he had another identity he liked to keep
secret.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.440
Demarchi was a committed anarchist.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:25.650
At some point in Allende’s childhood, the
boy and the Italian cobbler became friends.

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:29.640
Demarchi wound up teaching the teenager about
class oppression, worker’s rights, and the

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.210
failings of capitalism.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:35.820
Although Allende never fell for anarchism’s
charms, under Demarchi’s sway he became

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.050
a committed socialist.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:41.810
It was Allende who, in 1933, cofounded Chile’s
first Socialist party.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:45.900
Allende who, in 1945, became one of its first
elected Senators.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.760
It was while in office that he had his first
run in with Pinochet.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:49.760
Remember?

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.690
In that camp where Pinochet threatened to
shoot him?

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:54.480
Not that the Doctor dwelled much on the encounter.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:56.990
He was too busy running for president.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:01.770
In Chile’s 1952 election, Allende finished
an impressive… dead last.

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:06.040
He was even temporarily booted out his own
party for his abysmal campaign!

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:10.970
But you can’t keep a good socialist down
and, in 1958, Allende ran again.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:12.960
This time, he almost won.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.530
The result was painfully close.

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:19.560
The Conservative candidate squeaked home with
a mere 33,000 more votes than Allende.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:24.380
Allende’s team came away convinced that,
next time, they would win.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:27.950
Unfortunately, they hadn’t counted on Fidel
Castro.

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:33.610
On January 1, 1959, Fidel Castro overthrew
Cuba’s government and installed a Communist

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:34.610
dictatorship.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:38.590
Suddenly, the whole of Latin America was in
the grip of a red scare, and trying to win

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:45.120
elections as a socialist was like trying to
win Mr. Universe when you look like a Biographics

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:46.120
scriptwriter.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:49.590
Allende’s third presidential run, in 1964,
was a failure.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:54.070
The Doctor’s public admiration of Castro
left him a very distant second.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:55.950
Really, that should have been the end of it.

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:58.810
Allende had lost three elections now, get
the message already, dude!

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:01.520
But Allende didn’t get the message.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:06.610
Like Sisyphus marching his boulder up the
hill, Allende entered the 1970 election determined

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:07.680
to win.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.710
Against all the odds, he did.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:12.500
By 1970, Chile’s economy was struggling.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:15.470
There was widespread discontent, a hunger
for change.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:20.720
To top it all off, the rightwing parties split
the vote, leaving the way open for a leftwing

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:21.720
challenger.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.790
The result was Allende winning 36.6 percent
of the vote, a tiny mandate, but more than

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:27.919
anyone else.

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:31.480
The shock victory set alarm bells ringing
across the Americas.

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:35.850
In Washington, DC, the Nixon administration
demanded the CIA stop this upstart Marxist

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:37.500
from taking the presidency.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.639
Instead, they handed Allende the keys to the
Chilean White House.

00:07:41.639 --> 00:07:46.760
On October 25, 1970, a bunch of CIA-backed
rightwing paramilitaries tried to kidnap a

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:51.560
pro-Allende Chilean general, likely to clear
the way for a coup.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:55.720
But they bungled the operation, killing the
general and rallying the Chilean public, the

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:59.760
Chilean establishment, even the Chilean military
to Allende’s side.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.210
Suddenly, the Chilean Congress’s attempts
to block Allende’s inauguration were dead

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:05.210
in the water.

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:09.780
On November 5, 1970, Doctor Salvador Allende
was sworn in as Chile’s president; the first

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:14.390
democratically-elected Marxist head of state
in Latin American history.

00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:18.870
Barely was the inauguration ceremony over
than the CIA began plotting to make sure he

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:26.550
was also the last.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:30.080
The Battle for Chile
OK, so we’re up to 1970 now, a full 22 years

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:35.120
since we last saw Pinochet, guarding an internment
camp and threatening to shoot senators.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.050
That’s a heck of a long time.

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:40.490
What’s the old donkey been up to all these
years?

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:42.460
The answer is: nothing.

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:48.560
We can’t stress enough how the most intriguing
part of Pinochet’s life is how utterly destined

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.750
for obscurity he seemed to be.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:55.580
In 1970, he was a 55 year old career army
man whose only contribution to world history

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:59.360
had been once threatening to shoot the future
president of Chile.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:03.930
The one thing that pointed towards his future
notoriety was how, in 1971, he was placed

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.180
in charge of the Santiago army garrison.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:10.320
Y’know, the ideal post to have if you ever
wanted to send an army marching on the Chilean

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:11.320
capital.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:13.720
Not that Pinochet currently had any reason
to do so.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:19.180
Nor would’ve he ever had, were it not for
the third key player in our game.

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:20.570
The CIA.

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:26.450
Up in Washington, DC, the Nixon Whitehouse
was tearing its hair out at Allende’s election.

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:30.150
The CIA was ordered to “make the (Chilean)
economy scream.”

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:32.850
American money flowed into rightwing Chilean
propaganda.

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:33.890
Into sabotage attempts.

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:35.680
Into aborted coups.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.550
To be fair, Allende was a scary prospect for
US and Chilean elites.

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:44.810
With barely a third of the vote, he was nationalizing
everything in sight.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:49.080
Still, every move Allende made was in line
with the Chilean constitution.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:54.089
A socialist he may have been; a burgeoning
tyrant he was not.

00:09:54.089 --> 00:09:58.000
Keep that in mind as his tragic, bloody story
plays out.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.140
By 1972, Allende’s government was widely
unpopular.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:06.050
In an attempt to shore up support, the Socialist
president invited military men into his cabinet.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:08.240
Among them was Carlos Prats.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.970
The leading constitutionalist in the Chilean
Army, Prats wasn’t happy with Allende, but

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:16.560
he was even less happy with the idea the army
should remove him from power.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:21.010
So long as Prats was running the army, there
was no chance of a coup in Chile.

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:27.060
We know this because on 29 June, 1973, a bunch
of disaffected officers tried to stage one.

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:31.610
Known as the Tanquetazo because most of those
involved attacked the President’s residence

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:36.190
– La Moneda – in tanks, the coup was a
monumental failure.

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:37.850
Nobody flocked to the plotters’ banner.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:42.250
When Pinochet got wind, he made sure his garrison
came out on Allende’s side.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:45.710
There’s actually footage from this era that
appeared in the documentary The Battle of

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:51.040
Chile of Salvador Allende striding down the
streets of Santiago in the coup’s immediate

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:54.339
aftermath, Augusto Pinochet loyally at his
side.

00:10:54.339 --> 00:11:00.100
It was this very moment that convinced Carlos
Prats that Pinochet was on the Constitutionalist

00:11:00.100 --> 00:11:01.100
side.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:04.000
So when Prats resigned a month later, he made
sure Pinochet replaced him.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:10.300
And so it was that, on August 22, 1973, Augusto
Pinochet became head of the Chilean armed

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:11.300
forces.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.940
When most Chileans heard this, they were kinda
like “what?

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:15.620
That donkey?”

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.100
Even Pinochet’s wife thought he was joking.

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:19.720
But it was no joke.

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:25.410
Prats had just promoted the most unremarkable
man in Chile to a position of remarkable power.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:29.680
It was a decision that would soon destroy
him.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:36.380
Democracy Burns
Many years later, long after Salvador Allende

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:40.270
was dead, Pinochet would boast that he’d
been plotting a coup against the Socialist

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:41.770
for more than a year.

00:11:41.770 --> 00:11:46.070
But other sources paint a picture not of a
cunning man waiting for his chance, but a

00:11:46.070 --> 00:11:49.480
nobody who accidentally wound up heading a
conspiracy.

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:52.700
On September 8, Chile’s other generals confronted
Pinochet.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:58.430
While no record exists of the conversation,
it seems they basically said “look, we’re

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:03.120
gonna overthrow Allende, and you too if you
stand in our way.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.020
Now, are you in or out?”

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:07.930
It turned out Pinochet was way in.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:14.480
On September 11, 1973, the residents of Santiago
awoke to the earthshaking thud of explosions.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:18.170
Across the capital, military fighter jets
were bombing La Moneda, each pass causing

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:22.170
new plumes of smoke to spiral up towards the
clear blue sky.

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:26.850
Imagine watching on TV as the Whitehouse is
consumed by flames, knowing those burning

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:28.110
it are American soldiers.

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:31.260
That’s what millions of Chileans felt that
fateful morning.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:35.709
In a great historical irony, Allende’s first
reaction as the coup got underway was to mutter

00:12:35.709 --> 00:12:38.560
"I wonder what they have done with poor Pinochet”.

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:43.750
He had no way of knowing that the last army
man he thought was loyal was directing the

00:12:43.750 --> 00:12:46.350
bombs raining down around him.

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:48.770
Allende’s last stand is today legendary.

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:53.760
Armed with a Kalashnikov given to him by Fidel
Castro, he and his entourage managed to hold

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:55.820
off the Chilean army for hours.

00:12:55.820 --> 00:12:58.959
But there was only one way this could ever
end.

00:12:58.959 --> 00:13:04.100
As soldiers swarmed the flaming ruins of La
Moneda, Doctor Salvador Allende shot himself

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:05.250
through the head.

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.070
He was 65.

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:11.110
Out on the streets of Santiago, leftists,
protestors, artists and intellectuals were

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:15.920
all being rounded up and marched to the internment
camps that were springing up across the city.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:20.260
Most notoriously, this included the National
Stadium, where dissidents were tortured in

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:24.320
locker rooms before being dragged out onto
the pitch and shot through the head.

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:28.890
At the same time, announcements were going
out that Chile was now under the control of

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:32.709
a four man military junta, headed by General
Pinochet.

00:13:32.709 --> 00:13:36.850
As the shockwaves of the coup spread out,
the old Constitutionalist general Carlos Prats

00:13:36.850 --> 00:13:40.500
slipped out of Santiago for exile in Argentina.

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:45.580
Yet the man who had made Pinochet wouldn’t
be able to outrun his erstwhile friend for

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:46.580
long.

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:51.730
On September 30, 1974, a car bomb killed Prats
and his wife in Buenos Aires.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:55.480
It’s thought Pinochet himself ordered the
assassination.

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:59.140
Back in Santiago, the junta instituted a reign
of repression.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:00.960
Political parties were banned.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.180
Censorship put in place.

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:04.230
All leftist media shut down.

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.390
Around 80,000 people were arrested.

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:09.410
Another 200,000 were forced into exile.

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:11.720
Not that the junta itself lasted long.

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:16.981
On June 27, 1974, just ten months after the
coup, Pinochet used his clout with the Army

00:14:16.981 --> 00:14:19.160
to force the other junta leaders to step aside.

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:23.520
In their place, he had himself elevated to
Supreme Chief of the Nation.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:27.510
The long tradition of democratic rule in Chile
were now at an end.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:34.300
For the next 16 years, the Donkey would rule
his nation as its undisputed king.

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:42.269
Days of Terror
From the get-go, the regime of Augusto Pinochet

00:14:42.269 --> 00:14:44.970
marked itself out with its sheer brutality.

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:49.751
One of Pinochet’s first acts was to order
a mop up operation against leftists who still

00:14:49.751 --> 00:14:51.010
remained in Chile.

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:56.300
Known as the Caravan of Death, this unit traveled
the vast length of Chile’s deserts and wastelands,

00:14:56.300 --> 00:15:00.840
flying into small towns and executing anyone
who opposed military rule.

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:03.830
All told, 97 Chileans were murdered by the
Caravan.

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:08.330
In some cases, they were flown far out over
the Pacific in helicopters and simply hurled

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:09.330
to their fates.

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:13.030
Meanwhile, those who’d been arrested in
the aftermath of the coup were funneled into

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:14.240
concentration camps.

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:19.310
Out in the dry Atacama Desert, old saltpeter
works were turned into centers where leftists

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:23.680
were worked to death, or executed by being
blown up with dynamite.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:26.870
Similar camps appeared in the frozen wastes
of Patagonia.

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:30.620
But the worst of the new regime’s torture
centers was right in the capital.

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:34.010
The Villa Grimaldi was once a grand old colonial
house.

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:37.450
Now it became a monument to mankind’s worst
impulses.

00:15:37.450 --> 00:15:41.560
At the still-awful, but normal end of the
scale, prisoners there were suffocated to

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:43.820
the point of death and then released.

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:48.250
At the nastier end, the teenage daughters
of regime critics were kidnapped and used

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:52.100
as practice for soldiers learning the ropes
of electroshock torture.

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:56.709
At the most deranged end of all, female prisoners
were forced to have intercourse with animals

00:15:56.709 --> 00:15:58.670
while their guards watched and laughed.

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:00.899
It was a Grand Guignol of horror.

00:16:00.899 --> 00:16:03.300
Nor was it confined just to Chile.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:08.770
In November, 1975, Pinochet’s secret police
– the DINA – made contact with the dictatorships

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:14.870
of Brazil, Paraguay, and Uruguay – later
including Argentina after the March 1976 military

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:15.870
coup.

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:17.839
Together, they came up with something called
Operation Condor.

00:16:17.839 --> 00:16:21.089
Its goal was to assassinate every single leftist
on the continent.

00:16:21.089 --> 00:16:26.070
The idea was that the DINA would pass information
on Chilean dissidents to the other Condor

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:27.310
nations, and vise versa.

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:29.690
They would then “disappear” one another’s
enemies.

00:16:29.690 --> 00:16:35.160
For the 60,000 people killed by Condor, that
meant being dropped into deep lakes or rivers,

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:39.200
or shot and buried in remote mass graves no-one
would ever find.

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:41.959
Sometimes, though, it meant a more dramatic
end.

00:16:41.959 --> 00:16:47.260
On September 21, 1976, Salvador Allende’s
former Foreign Minister, Orlando Letelier,

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:51.399
got into his car in Washington, DC, where
he’d been living in exile since the coup.

00:16:51.399 --> 00:16:54.550
With him was a 25-year old US citizen, Ronni
Moffitt.

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:59.810
At 09:30 am, a bomb concealed in the car exploded,
killing both occupants.

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:04.270
Even the American capital seemingly wasn’t
safe from the DINA’s reach.

00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:07.579
Yet, this terror only strengthened Pinochet’s
grip on power.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:12.059
As the 1980s dawned, the Chilean tyrant rammed
through a new constitution giving himself

00:17:12.059 --> 00:17:13.799
8 year terms as president.

00:17:13.799 --> 00:17:19.129
Come 1988, there would be a Yes or No referendum
on whether Pinochet could keep power.

00:17:19.129 --> 00:17:22.639
Until then, he would be the one man ruler
of Chile.

00:17:22.639 --> 00:17:25.889
Chile’s dictatorship was here to stay.

00:17:25.889 --> 00:17:33.289
The Two “Miracles”
By this point, you may be wondering why such

00:17:33.289 --> 00:17:36.489
a perverse regime wasn’t an international
pariah.

00:17:36.489 --> 00:17:41.799
The reasons can be summed up in a single phrase:
“the miracle of Chile.”

00:17:41.799 --> 00:17:45.539
That was the name given by the free market
economist Milton Friedman to what happened

00:17:45.539 --> 00:17:47.830
in Chile after Pinochet seized power.

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:51.519
Under successive governments, Chile’s economy
had been badly struggling.

00:17:51.519 --> 00:17:55.480
During Allende’s years in office, things
had only gotten worse – although its debatable

00:17:55.480 --> 00:18:01.679
whether that was due to leftwing policies,
or due to the CIA constantly trying to instigate

00:18:01.679 --> 00:18:02.679
a coup.

00:18:02.679 --> 00:18:06.539
When Pinochet took over, he decided to try
a new route, one mapped out by the Chicago

00:18:06.539 --> 00:18:07.539
Boys.

00:18:07.539 --> 00:18:11.549
The Chicago Boys were native Chileans who’d
studied in Chicago under Friedman.

00:18:11.549 --> 00:18:17.859
Suddenly elevated by the regime to key positions,
they were tasked with transforming the economy.

00:18:17.859 --> 00:18:18.859
Transform it, they did.

00:18:18.859 --> 00:18:23.529
In the second half of the 1970s, Chile became
a laboratory for perhaps the most extreme

00:18:23.529 --> 00:18:26.889
free market economic policies in history.

00:18:26.889 --> 00:18:32.679
Everything that wasn’t bolted down was privatized,
everything opened up to the market, and businesses

00:18:32.679 --> 00:18:36.559
given unfettered access to all parts of Chilean
life.

00:18:36.559 --> 00:18:42.629
The result was a wild rollercoaster ride that
saw both the highest inflation rate in Chile’s

00:18:42.629 --> 00:18:46.929
history, and one of the worst one-year recessions
on record.

00:18:46.929 --> 00:18:51.110
But, by the time 1988 arrived, the wild ride
was over.

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:54.619
Chile’s economy was the most stable in Latin
America.

00:18:54.619 --> 00:18:55.619
Chile was rich.

00:18:55.619 --> 00:18:59.350
Pinochet’s supporters still point to this
as his signature achievement.

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:03.999
It certainly won the regime friends, with
Margaret Thatcher openly talking of her admiration

00:19:03.999 --> 00:19:04.999
for Pinochet.

00:19:04.999 --> 00:19:07.899
But even here, we should sound a note of caution.

00:19:07.899 --> 00:19:12.909
Chile today is successful, but it also has
one of the highest levels of inequality in

00:19:12.909 --> 00:19:13.909
the world.

00:19:13.909 --> 00:19:19.080
The Chicago Boys may have made Chile rich,
but they did so while eviscerating the poorest

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:20.270
in society.

00:19:20.270 --> 00:19:22.649
Come 1988, Pinochet was riding high.

00:19:22.649 --> 00:19:27.059
The economy was doing great, all his enemies
were dead, and he was secure in power.

00:19:27.059 --> 00:19:31.369
So when it came time to hold that referendum
we mentioned earlier, the one on keeping Pinochet

00:19:31.369 --> 00:19:34.980
as dictator, Pinochet went ahead and held
it.

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:38.350
Despite all the abuses, he thought he really
was popular.

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:41.809
How could the hero who’d delivered Chile
from socialism lose?

00:19:41.809 --> 00:19:47.940
But if Pinochet thought he was a hero, Chile’s
electorate was about to prove they still saw

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:49.509
him as a donkey.

00:19:49.509 --> 00:19:53.870
On October 5, 1988, the plebiscite returned
its shock result.

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:57.419
The pro-Pinochet side had scraped a mere 44%
of the vote.

00:19:57.419 --> 00:20:00.830
The anti-Pinochet side had won by 12 points.

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:04.509
The next day, an outraged Pinochet called
his generals to his offices and declared he

00:20:04.509 --> 00:20:08.559
would never stand down, that the vote was
a fix, that it was all a mistake.

00:20:08.559 --> 00:20:11.259
But the generals knew something the old donkey
didn’t.

00:20:11.259 --> 00:20:15.889
They knew the US was already piling on the
pressure, that Pinochet would have to honor

00:20:15.889 --> 00:20:18.919
the result, or face international consequences.

00:20:18.919 --> 00:20:21.470
One by one, they refused to back Pinochet.

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:29.970
Isolated, the dictator had no choice but to
publicly announce he would step down in two

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:34.190
years.

00:20:34.190 --> 00:20:47.259
The next year, in 1989, the first presidential
election since 1970 was held in Chile.

00:20:47.259 --> 00:20:51.989
The center left candidate, Patricio Aylwin,
romped home with over 55% of the vote.

00:20:51.989 --> 00:20:54.590
Suddenly, the age of oppression was over.

00:20:54.590 --> 00:20:59.759
When Aylwin was inaugurated on March 11, 1990,
democracy finally returned to Chile.

00:20:59.759 --> 00:21:02.440
Of course, Pinochet didn’t just return to
civilian life.

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:06.320
He remained head of the armed forces, and
was later gifted not just the post “senator

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:09.190
for life”, but immunity from prosecution.

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:14.880
But if the Donkey thought he’d have an easy
retirement, he had another thing coming.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:20.700
Justice At Last?

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:25.249
On October 17, 1998, Augusto Pinochet was
visiting London for minor surgery on his back.

00:21:25.249 --> 00:21:29.350
Ever since he’d stepped down as Chile’s
leader, 8 long years ago, the former dictator

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:31.200
had grown fond of the British capital.

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:35.549
Thanks to Chile’s clandestine help to Britain
during the Falklands War, he thought the British

00:21:35.549 --> 00:21:36.879
had grown fond of him, too.

00:21:36.879 --> 00:21:40.740
Well, the British were about to give him a
rude awakening.

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:45.179
That day, as the 83-year old Pinochet lay
in hospital, he was arrested by officers from

00:21:45.179 --> 00:21:46.779
London’s Met Police.

00:21:46.779 --> 00:21:51.679
Seven days earlier, a Spanish judge known
as Baltasar Garzón had issued an international

00:21:51.679 --> 00:21:53.570
arrest warrant for Pinochet.

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:57.650
Under Spanish law, serious crimes against
Spanish citizens can be charged in Spanish

00:21:57.650 --> 00:22:00.999
courts no matter what jurisdiction they are
committed in.

00:22:00.999 --> 00:22:06.080
Several Spanish citizens had been abducted
and tortured in Chile during the regime, and

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:07.549
now it was payback time.

00:22:07.549 --> 00:22:10.340
The arrest was like a lightning bolt to the
international community.

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:13.950
Suddenly, the eyes of Pinochet’s many victims
were all trained on London.

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:18.629
We wish we could now wrap up this video with
“and then Pinochet was extradited to Madrid,

00:22:18.629 --> 00:22:23.559
where the judge found him guilty and sentenced
him to a bazillion years in prison,” but,

00:22:23.559 --> 00:22:25.850
sadly, that’s not what happened.

00:22:25.850 --> 00:22:30.590
Although British Parliament ruled international
immunity did not apply in cases of torture,

00:22:30.590 --> 00:22:34.629
the establishment still closed ranks to protect
a foreign leader.

00:22:34.629 --> 00:22:38.539
On the Conservative side, Margaret Thatcher
made an impassioned plea for Pinochet to be

00:22:38.539 --> 00:22:39.539
freed.

00:22:39.539 --> 00:22:43.399
On the Labour Party side, the Home Secretary,
Jack Straw, declared Pinochet was too ill

00:22:43.399 --> 00:22:44.549
to stand trial.

00:22:44.549 --> 00:22:49.789
On March 3, 2000, Pinochet was wheeled onto
a plane from London to Chile, looking like

00:22:49.789 --> 00:22:51.059
he was at death’s door.

00:22:51.059 --> 00:22:55.509
When he touched down in Santiago, the dictator
triumphantly stood up from his wheelchair

00:22:55.509 --> 00:22:57.630
and smugly waved at his supporters.

00:22:57.630 --> 00:23:02.070
As is so often the case with history, the
monster had escaped unscathed.

00:23:02.070 --> 00:23:05.460
However, there is still a postscript to this
story.

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:09.340
While the Spanish warrant never went anywhere,
at home in Chile, lawmakers were starting

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:12.489
to reassess the idea of lifetime immunity.

00:23:12.489 --> 00:23:16.739
In 2004, a state commission on crimes of the
dictatorship dropped a shocking report that

00:23:16.739 --> 00:23:22.720
revealed over 35,000 people had been tortured
by the regime, and over 3,000 murdered.

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:26.880
That same year, Chilean lawmakers lifted Pinochet’s
immunity over the Caravan of Death killings,

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:31.600
and the car bomb assassination of the Constitutionalist
general, Carlos Prats.

00:23:31.600 --> 00:23:35.470
One year later, in 2005, Pinochet was placed
under house arrest.

00:23:35.470 --> 00:23:37.529
It was days after his 90th birthday.

00:23:37.529 --> 00:23:40.590
Over the next year, more and more allegations
came to light.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:43.760
Evidence of colossal levels of embezzlement
was presented.

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:48.429
In 2006, Pinochet was formally charged with
kidnapping, torture, and murder.

00:23:48.429 --> 00:23:51.799
Sadly, the stubborn donkey once again escaped
justice.

00:23:51.799 --> 00:23:56.729
On December 10, 2006, Pinochet died of a heart
attack at his home in Santiago.

00:23:56.729 --> 00:24:00.210
Just days earlier, he’d been told he was
fit to stand trial.

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:04.489
Today, the Chilean state has begun to slowly
account for the crimes committed during Pinochet’s

00:24:04.489 --> 00:24:05.489
tenure.

00:24:05.489 --> 00:24:09.470
Over 40,000 living victims have been identified
and provided with compensation.

00:24:09.470 --> 00:24:13.870
Around thirteen hundred former officers and
soldiers have been tried for crimes against

00:24:13.870 --> 00:24:14.940
humanity.

00:24:14.940 --> 00:24:19.840
Chile may still be haunted by the shadow of
Augusto Pinochet, but at least history is

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:23.080
now able to judge him for the monster he was.

00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:45.889
He may have escaped justice in his lifetime,
but Pinochet’s crimes will go down in history.

